             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary>
               <p>Let \(d(u) &lt; d(v)\). When \(u\) is discovered, \(v\) is either undiscovered or discovered already. If \(v\) is already discovered at \(d(u)\), then \(d(v) &lt; d(u)\), a contradiction. So \(v\) is undiscovered at \(d(u)\). If \(v\) were not discovered before \(f(u)\), the loop over the neighbours of \(u\) would reach \(v\) and discover it during \(u\)'s call, so \(v\) would be a descendant of \(u\). If \(v\) were discovered after \(f(u)\), the edge \(\{u, v\}\) would be scanned from \(u\) before \(f(u)\), which would discover \(v\) during \(u\)'s call. Either way \(v\) is a descendant of \(u\).</p>
